  "place": "Kazan, Russia",
  "nameOrigin": "From Ruthenia, a Medieval Latin name for Russia, where the platinum ore came from.",
  "source": "pubchem",
  "note": "PubChem's YearDiscovered (1827) refers to Osann's claim (1828 in CIAAW); Klaus showed that Osann's material was impure and obtained the metal itself. Śniadecki's 1807 claim had been withdrawn."
 },
 "prose": {
  "text": "Ruthenium is a hard, silvery platinum-group metal, recovered as a by-product of platinum and nickel refining and named after Ruthenia, a Latin name for Russia, because it was first isolated from platinum ore from the Ural Mountains. A pinch goes a long way: about a tenth of a per cent of ruthenium makes titanium far more resistant to corrosion, and small additions harden platinum and palladium for hard-wearing electrical contacts. Its atom breaks the filling rule: the measured ground configuration is [Kr] 4d⁷ 5s¹, not the predicted 4d⁶ 5s², giving four unpaired electrons and the ground term ⁵F₅.",
  "sources": [
   "pubchem",
   "nist_gsie"
  ]
 }
}
